        "source_notes": [
          {
            "dataset": "soter_polygons",
            "source_code": "cecs",
            "source_notes": "Values as stored in the SOTER database of each dataset, represented at 7 significant digits (binary storage tails removed); the extracted value is kept unchanged in the raw layer. The 4-letter column codes are not defined in any retrieved ISRIC document: the manual items cited here are matched to the column by its name and by the fill rates of the dataset reports (inference documented in docs/inventory/soter-source-fields.md). CEC of the soil at pH 7.0, cmol(c) kg-1 (M95 item 107, p.56; M13 item 127, p.74). SOTERSAF Angola: 'the barium chloride method, buffered with triethanolamine at pH 8.1, has been used for all samples' (ISRIC SOTERSAF report 2003, p.12). 7 values exceed 200 (max 1,340, Senegal/Gambia); published as in the source. Malawi: the compilers divided CEC and exchangeable bases of 13 districts by a factor 10 and discarded remaining implausible values (ISRIC Report 2016/01, p.25-26). Kenya (KENSOTER) and Upper Tana give values for profiles with 'syn' in PRID: 'synthetic profiles' for which 'all 18 parameter estimates have been derived via taxotransfer or using expert estimates' (ISRIC Report 2004/01, p.10, p.14), 'virtual profiles, for which there are no measured soil data' (ISRIC Report 2010/09, p.11); every row of such a profile carries source_profile_type (synthetic or virtual)."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "column": "clay_content",
        "property_key": "clay_content",
        "numeric": true,
        "units": [
          "% w/w"
        ],
        "source_codes": [
          "clpc"
        ],
        "horizon_count": 5,
        "multi_value_cells": 0,
        "methods": {},
        "source_notes": [
          {
            "dataset": "soter_polygons",
            "source_code": "clpc",
            "source_notes": "Values as stored in the SOTER database of each dataset, represented at 7 significant digits (binary storage tails removed); the extracted value is kept unchanged in the raw layer. The 4-letter column codes are not defined in any retrieved ISRIC document: the manual items cited here are matched to the column by its name and by the fill rates of the dataset reports (inference documented in docs/inventory/soter-source-fields.md). Weight % of the fine earth (M95 items 82-84, p.54: sand 2.0-0.05, silt 0.05-0.002, clay < 0.002 mm; M13 items 102-104, p.71). SOTERSAF: Angola limits differ (report 2003, p.12); Botswana used hydrometer and pipette methods (p.15). Sand + silt + clay lies outside 98-102 in 708 of 22,372 complete triplets (South Africa 390 of 1,605, SOTERSAF 240 of 3,276, Senegal/Gambia 32 of 299, Nepal 9 of 526); 28 horizons have one or two of the three fractions; published as in the source. The SOTER column CLAY is the clay mineralogy (a horizon attribute), not the clay content. Kenya (KENSOTER) and Upper Tana give values for profiles with 'syn' in PRID: 'synthetic profiles' for which 'all 18 parameter estimates have been derived via taxotransfer or using expert estimates' (ISRIC Report 2004/01, p.10, p.14), 'virtual profiles, for which there are no measured soil data' (ISRIC Report 2010/09, p.11); every row of such a profile carries source_profile_type (synthetic or virtual)."
          }
        ]
